deferred-debugger-compiler-two-bodies — Phase 8: compiler two-bodies discipline
================================================================================

See also:
  clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-debugger-implementation-plan.org (Phase 8)
  clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-debugger-spec.org (§5, §13)

DEFERRED — gated on the clautolisp compiler-to-CL, which does not yet exist.
UPDATE 2026-09-29: the gate is lifted -- the compiler exists (compiler.issue,
slices from 2.0.0). This is now TODO, workable on Linux.

Context
-------

clautolisp is currently an interpreter, so the v1 debugger uses the
`*debug-mode*` interpreter instrumentation variant (spec §11.3, §13 "v1").
The full two-bodies discipline (Raskin) with call-site entry-point dispatch
(Fjeld) becomes available only when the compiler-to-CL lands. The debug
protocol (Part III) and all three UIs are UNCHANGED across the migration
(spec §13), so this is purely an instrumentation-backend swap.

Scope (when unblocked)
----------------------

- Emit two CL bodies per AutoLISP defun: `foo/ordinary` (optimised, no poll
  points) and `foo/debug` (instrumented), behind a function-entries vector
  (spec §5.1).
- Disable across-poll-point optimisations in the debug body (spec §5.2): no
  CSE / dead-code elim of sub-form results / LICM / TCO / type-inference
  propagation across poll points.
- Call-site dispatch: ordinary code calls ordinary entry points, debug code
  calls debug entry points; debugging-ness propagates down the call chain
  (spec §5). Emit `function-debug-metadata` from the compiler (spec §7).
- Keep the interpreter available as a fallback (low optimisation /
  macroexpansion debugging).

Acceptance (when unblocked)
---------------------------

- Production (ordinary) code pays zero instrumentation cost; entering a debug
  entry point keeps the whole call chain instrumented; same UI behaviour as
  the interpreter path.

Closed (2026-10-06, clautolisp 2.2.206)
---------------------------------------

An audit against this scope found it delivered, in a different encoding:

- Two bodies per function: DONE as slots on the usubr (compiled-body,
  compiled-instrumented-body; compile-usubr / compile-instrumented-usubr),
  not as named foo/ordinary + foo/debug behind a function-entries vector --
  AutoLISP resolves a callee by symbol at every call (REPL redefinition), so
  a call site cannot bind an entry point at compile time anyway.
- Call-site dispatch and propagation down the call chain: SUPERSEDED by
  design note DN-2 (clautolisp-debugger-design-notes.org): the body is
  chosen per callee for the whole session; every function is instrumented on
  first call under a session, so the whole chain is debuggable, including
  through uninstrumented library code (test
  instrumented-callee-debugged-through-uninstrumented-caller).
- Same UI behaviour compiled or interpreted: DONE (compiler.issue slice 7;
  make test-debug-compiled runs the debug corpus with every instrumented body
  compiled -- 603 this run, 0 failures).
- Ordinary code pays zero instrumentation: DONE (no poll points; one test
  of *debugging* per call).
- Interpreter as fallback: DONE.

What this closure added:
- the debugging body is compiled at (optimize (debug 3)) -- spec 5.2 stated
  as a policy, and no tail call merged out of the debugger's backtrace
  (instrumented-fork-lambda-form; test the-debug-fork-is-compiled-at-debug-3);
- a .lap carries its source positions, registered on load only under
  source tracking, so a .lap function is debuggable by line (test
  an-artefact-keeps-the-source-positions-for-the-debugger);
- the debugger spec: an "as built" note in section 5 and section 13
  rewritten to the implemented design.

Found and filed separately: debug-lambda-in-a-loop-reinstrumented.
